          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My HbA1c came back at 7.2%. What does that mean, what target does guidance describe, and which code would type 2 diabetes be recorded under?",
          "answer": "An HbA1c value of 7.2% is above the general target described in diabetes association guidance (guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets).\n\nFor many non-pregnant adults, a reasonable goal for HbA1c is below 7 percent, though this target is individualized (guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets). For some individuals, a more stringent goal, such as below 6.5 percent, might be considered if it can be achieved without harm (guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets). You should discuss your specific target with your clinician (guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets).\n\nGuidance on target HbA1c levels for type 2 diabetes varies (National diabetes association standards of care, guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets). Some guidelines suggest 7% as a default target, while others emphasize an individualized range as primary, avoiding a single headline figure to prevent rigid application (National diabetes association standards of care, guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets).\n\nGlycemic control is one of several targets in managing diabetes; other important factors for cardiovascular outcomes include blood pressure control, lipid management, and smoking cessation (National diabetes association standards of care, guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets). HbA1c results can also be unreliable if there are conditions that alter red blood cell turnover, such as hemoglobin variants, recent transfusion, iron-deficiency anemia, pregnancy, hemolysis, or advanced kidney disease, in which cases glucose-based criteria are used instead (National diabetes association standards of care, guideline-type-2-diabetes-screening).\n\nType 2 diabetes mellitus is recorded under the ICD-10-CM code E11, which is found in Chapter 4 covering endocrine, nutritional, and metabolic diseases (coding-type-2-diabetes-e11).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
